      "title": "Simulation on Plasma Diffusion of Pulse Current Rising Process in Triggered Vacuum Switch",
      "abstract": "The triggered vacuum switch (TVS) is an important component in a pulsed power facility, directly affecting output power and control precision. Previous studies on TVS have primarily focused on trigger delay time; however, there has been little attention paid to the vacuum arc plasma diffusion process in TVS, despite its influence on the pulse current rising characteristics of the switch. The plasma diffusion processes in TVS are addressed in this paper using PIC-MCC simulation, along with their effects on current rise in TVS. The influences of electric and magnetic field distributions in the contact gap, as well as its length, on plasma diffusion are analyzed. The results indicate that the electric field in the contact gap significantly affects the speed of arc ignition and plasma diffusion, while higher electric field strengths accelerate the formation of stronger arc channels through faster plasma diffusion. The arc diffusion path and its stability are also influenced by the magnetic field distribution in the vacuum contact gap, primarily affecting the current rise rate. Furthermore, a shorter gap distance promotes the formation and development of vacuum arc channels, leading to a faster current rising process. Based on the simulations presented in this paper, suggestions for designing electrode structures in TVS are provided, offering a basis for developing vacuum switches with improved performance.",
