      "title": "Pulsed Power Plasma Applications Driven by Power Elctronic Switches and Resonant Converters",
      "abstract": "Pulsed power applications such as plasma ignition, pre-ionization and heating often require high power in either single pulse or sinusoidal burst. Traditional solutions are based on capacitor discharge using spark switches or vacuum tube amplifiers such as triodes. These devices considered to be robust and reliable. However, relatively low efficiency, limited availability, niche applications, low future investment in this technology, and the requirement for periodic maintenance are pushing the designers towards solid-state solutions. In recent years, the landscape of pulse-power generation has changed dramatically. The rapid development of power electronics, driven by data centers, renewable energy, and electric vehicles, has revolutionized the field, offering higher breakdown voltage, greater peak currents, faster switching times, repeatable results and relatively lower interference when comparing spark switch triggering to solid-state. The paper presents an overview of solidstate solutions and their integration into the pulsed-power applications, from single pulsed capacitor discharge to burst AC high current converters supporting frequencies from few kHz up to MHz region in millisecond time frame. Power trains are based on IGBTs and wide bandgap devices such as SiC FETs. Experiments are carried out to support the proposed technology.",
