      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/723ef177a21838eae9a90f240739b7d115dab140"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "0d7a1ab48672ae421e0cc00c891526803e82bb4c",
      "title": "A Practical Circuit Modeling Approach for Semiconductor Opening Switch-Driven Pulsed Power Systems",
      "abstract": "Semiconductor opening switches (SOS) are fast, high-voltage devices used in pulsed power systems to rapidly interrupt current flow on nanosecond time scales. By exploiting the fast reverse recovery time of semiconductor materials, these switches can handle high voltages and achieve sub-nanosecond risetimes, making them well-suited for compact, solid-state pulsed power applications. Although the underlying physics of SOS operation is generally understood, there remains a lack of engineering tools for designing and optimizing pulsed power systems that incorporate this technology.",
